# Clean Architecture Guidelines

You are a clean architecture expert who ensures the Wispen financial assistant follows proper architectural patterns, maintaining separation of concerns and clear dependency rules.

## Architecture Overview

- Implement a strict layered architecture with clear boundaries
- Enforce dependency rule: dependencies only point inward
- Ensure inner layers have no knowledge of outer layers
- Use interfaces to define boundaries between layers
- Apply dependency injection for all cross-layer dependencies
- Keep domain logic independent of frameworks and external services

## Layer Structure

### 1. Core (Domain) Layer

- Contains all business logic and rules
- Defines entities that represent core business concepts
- Implements use cases that orchestrate business operations
- Has no dependencies on external frameworks or libraries
- Uses pure TypeScript/JavaScript with minimal dependencies
- Defines interface contracts that outer layers must implement

### 2. Adapters Layer

- Implements interfaces defined by the domain layer
- Contains controllers for handling input (WhatsApp messages, API requests)
- Implements repositories for data access
- Acts as a bridge between domain and infrastructure
- Transforms data between domain entities and external formats
- Contains no business logic, only translation logic

### 3. Infrastructure Layer

- Provides concrete implementations of adapters
- Interacts with external systems (Supabase, OpenAI, WhatsApp)
- Manages configuration and environment setup
- Handles database connections and migrations
- Contains framework-specific code (Express.js)
- Implements technical concerns (logging, caching, security)

## Core Domain Implementation

### Entities

- Design entities to represent business objects (Usuario, Transaccion, MetaAhorro, LimiteGasto)
- Keep entities simple with minimal dependencies
- Implement business rules within entities when appropriate
- Use factories to create complex entities
- Avoid exposing implementation details

### Use Cases

- Implement one use case per business operation
- Use cases orchestrate entities to fulfill business requirements
- Keep use cases focused on a single responsibility
- Implement input and output ports (interfaces) for each use case
- Use DTOs for input/output boundary crossing

### Domain Services

- Implement domain services for operations that don't belong to a single entity
- Keep domain services stateless
- Inject required dependencies through constructors
- Use interfaces for dependencies from outer layers

## Adapter Implementation

### Controllers

- Create dedicated controllers for each input source (WhatsApp, API)
- Controllers translate external input to domain input
- Keep controllers thin by delegating to use cases
- Handle input validation and sanitization
- Return appropriate responses for different scenarios

### Repositories

- Implement repository pattern for data access
- Each repository focuses on a single domain entity
- Repositories translate between domain entities and database models
- Hide database implementation details from domain layer
- Use interfaces defined in domain layer

### Presenters

- Transform domain output to appropriate response format
- Handle formatting for different channels (WhatsApp text, interactive messages)
- Implement error formatting and translation
- Keep presentation logic separate from business logic

## Infrastructure Implementation

### External Services

- Implement service adapters for all external APIs (OpenAI, Mistral, WhatsApp)
- Handle authentication and connection management
- Implement retries and error handling
- Log interactions with external systems
- Provide mock implementations for testing

### Database Access

- Implement concrete repository classes using Prisma/Supabase
- Handle database-specific concerns (transactions, migrations)
- Implement efficient query patterns
- Manage database connections and pooling
- Handle database-specific errors

### Configuration

- Centralize configuration management
- Load environment variables securely
- Provide sensible defaults for all configuration
- Validate configuration at startup
- Implement different configurations for environments

## Cross-Cutting Concerns

### Dependency Injection

- Use constructor injection for all dependencies
- Create factory functions for complex object creation
- Avoid service locator pattern
- Initialize dependencies at composition root
- Use interfaces for all injected dependencies

### Error Handling

- Define domain-specific exceptions in core layer
- Transform technical errors to domain errors in adapters
- Implement consistent error handling across layers
- Provide meaningful error messages for users
- Log detailed errors for debugging

### Logging

- Implement logging through interfaces
- Inject logger dependencies where needed
- Log appropriate information at each layer
- Sanitize sensitive data before logging
- Use structured logging for easier analysis

## Testing Strategy

### Domain Layer Testing

- Focus on thorough unit testing of domain logic
- Test entities and use cases in isolation
- Use in-memory repositories for use case tests
- Test business rules and edge cases thoroughly
- Implement test factories for domain objects

### Adapter Layer Testing

- Test controllers with mocked use cases
- Test repositories with in-memory databases or mocks
- Focus on correct translation between layers
- Test error handling and edge cases
- Use integration tests for repository implementations

### Infrastructure Layer Testing

- Implement integration tests for external services
- Use test doubles for third-party services
- Test configuration loading and validation
- Test database migrations and schemas
- Focus on correct integration with external systems

## Directory Structure

- Organize code by layer first, then by feature
- Keep related code together within each layer
- Use consistent naming conventions across layers
- Separate interfaces from implementations
- Place shared utilities in dedicated directories

## Implementation Patterns

- Use TypeScript interfaces to define boundaries
- Implement pure functions where possible
- Use factories and builders for complex object creation
- Apply command pattern for use case implementation
- Use mapper pattern for data transformation
- Implement strategy pattern for varying behaviors 